        "id": 18,
        "name": "Junagarh Fort",
        "slug": "junagarh-fort",
        "city": "Bikaner",
        "state": "Rajasthan",
        "category": "Fort",
        "subcategory": "Ground Fort",
        "description": "Junagarh Fort is Rajasthan's only undefeated ground-level fort, built on flat desert land rather than a hill. Built with red sandstone and marble, it houses 37 palaces, temples and pavilions and has never been conquered in its history.",
        "history": "<p>Rising majestically in <strong>Bikaner</strong>, Rajasthan, <strong>Junagarh Fort</strong> is a timeless tribute to its founder <strong>Raja Rai Singh</strong>, who ordered its construction in <strong>1593 AD</strong>. Junagarh Fort is Rajasthan's only undefeated ground-level fort, built on flat desert land rather than a hill. Built with red sandstone and marble, it houses 37 palaces, temples and pavilions and has never been conquered in its history. Recognised today as Only undefeated ground-level fort in Rajasthan, this remarkable structure has earned its place among the most celebrated destinations in all of India, drawing scholars, historians, and travellers from every corner of the globe.</p>\n          \n          <h3>Junagarh Fort — Rajput-Mughal (Sandstone & Marble) Architecture at Its Finest</h3><p>What makes <strong>Junagarh Fort</strong> architecturally extraordinary is the way its <strong>Rajput-Mughal (Sandstone & Marble)</strong> design traditions have been applied with such precision and artistry. The builders who worked under the patronage of <strong>Raja Rai Singh</strong> were masters of their craft — every carved surface, every proportioned arch, and every perfectly planned courtyard speaks of decades of accumulated knowledge in stone-working and spatial design. The result continues to astonish architects and historians even by contemporary standards.</p>\n          \n          <p>The role of <strong>Junagarh Fort</strong> in shaping Bikaner's cultural and political landscape cannot be overstated. From the moment <strong>Raja Rai Singh</strong> unveiled it in <strong>1593 AD</strong>, this fort became inseparable from the identity of the entire region. It served as the stage for royal coronations, grand durbars, and important religious ceremonies that defined the rhythms of Rajputana life for centuries. Even in times of conflict, Junagarh Fort stood firm as a symbol of continuity and cultural resilience.</p>\n          \n          <h3>The Finest Features of Junagarh Fort</h3><p>The range of extraordinary sights at <strong>Junagarh Fort</strong> ensures that every visitor carries something unique away with them. The magnificent <strong>Anup Mahal (Gold Leaf Paintings)</strong> has long been the centrepiece of the entire complex — so remarkable that it alone has been the subject of scholarly studies and artistic tributes for decades. The equally impressive <strong>Phool Mahal</strong> showcases a different facet of the <strong>Rajput-Mughal (Sandstone & Marble)</strong> tradition that makes Junagarh Fort such a rich experience. The <strong>Chandra Mahal</strong> rounds off a trio of must-see attractions central to any thorough exploration of this iconic Bikaner landmark.</p>\n          \n          <h3>Planning Your Visit to Junagarh Fort</h3><p>Today, <strong>Junagarh Fort</strong> welcomes visitors from across India and around the world who come to experience the enduring magic of Bikaner's most cherished heritage. Visit morning for fewer crowds. Golden light in evening makes fort photogenic. Plan to spend at least <strong>2-3 Hours</strong> to do justice to everything this remarkable fort has to offer — rushing through would mean missing the countless details that collectively make it one of Rajasthan's greatest treasures.</p>",
