        "id": 19,
        "name": "Chittorgarh Fort",
        "slug": "chittorgarh-fort",
        "city": "Chittorgarh",
        "state": "Rajasthan",
        "category": "Fort",
        "subcategory": "Hill Fort",
        "description": "Chittorgarh Fort is India's largest fort by area (280 hectares) and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The fort has witnessed 3 major sieges and is the symbol of Rajput pride, sacrifice and valor. Rani Padmini's Jauhar (self-immolation) took place here.",
        "history": "<p>Few places in India carry the weight of history as powerfully as <strong>Chittorgarh Fort</strong>, a magnificent fort in <strong>Chittorgarh</strong> commissioned by <strong>Mauryan Rulers (expanded by Rajputs)</strong> in <strong>7th Century AD</strong>. Chittorgarh Fort is India's largest fort by area (280 hectares) and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The fort has witnessed 3 major sieges and is the symbol of Rajput pride, sacrifice and valor. Rani Padmini's Jauhar (self-immolation) took place here. Since its inception, this extraordinary structure has stood as a testament to Rajasthan's unparalleled architectural heritage, inviting visitors and scholars to unravel the stories etched into every corridor, courtyard, and carved facade.</p>\n          \n          <h3>The Rajputana (Sandstone) Legacy at Chittorgarh Fort</h3><p>The <strong>Rajputana (Sandstone)</strong> architectural language of <strong>Chittorgarh Fort</strong> is immediately apparent to any visitor who passes through its grand entrance. Shaped by the cultural vision of <strong>Mauryan Rulers (expanded by Rajputs)</strong>, the monument demonstrates a profound understanding of space, light, and proportion that was centuries ahead of its time. Intricate stone carvings, delicately perforated screens, and thoughtfully positioned water features all contribute to an architectural composition that speaks to both the aesthetic sensibility and practical intelligence of its creators.</p>\n          \n          <p>Few landmarks in Rajasthan carry as rich a historical narrative as <strong>Chittorgarh Fort</strong>. Since its foundation in <strong>7th Century AD</strong>, this fort has stood witness to the rise and fall of empires, the flourishing of arts and trade, and the gradual evolution of <strong>Chittorgarh</strong> from a medieval stronghold into a vibrant modern city. Successive rulers who added their contributions left not just architectural imprints but also cultural legacies — traditions, artistic styles, and governance practices — that continue to shape the spirit of Chittorgarh and its people to this day.</p>\n          \n          <h3>Key Attractions at Chittorgarh Fort</h3><p>Among the many wonders at <strong>Chittorgarh Fort</strong>, the <strong>Vijay Stambha (Victory Tower, 15th Century)</strong> consistently stands out as one of the most memorable experiences. The <strong>Kirti Stambha (Tower of Fame)</strong> is equally impressive, offering a window into the extraordinary craftsmanship that defines this monument. Visitors also delight in the opportunity to exploring the historic architecture and layouts, as well as the experience of taking scenic photographs of the ancient structures. These encounters combine to create a visit that is rich in sensory impressions, historical insight, and lasting memories.</p>\n          \n          <h3>Visiting Chittorgarh Fort Today</h3><p>A visit to <strong>Chittorgarh Fort</strong> in <strong>Chittorgarh</strong> is an experience that resonates long after you leave its precincts. The monument's careful preservation ensures that visitors encounter the same splendours that have awed travellers for centuries, now complemented by modern amenities and expert guided interpretation. Hire a vehicle or guide inside - the fort is enormous (280 hectares). Full day needed. Allow <strong>5-6 Hours</strong> to explore at a comfortable pace — this is a destination where every minute of exploration is richly rewarded.</p>",
